{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794669492929,"sessionID":"ses_e255ff46fffexTncZnylD3HfiJ","part":{"id":"prt_1daa33ac1001TOxwtl51HB1PQZ","sessionID":"ses_e255ff46fffexTncZnylD3HfiJ","messageID":"msg_1daa3101e0015kTAkAsMQLyy8m","type":"step-start","snapshot":"17835a4dc7b7f623f0c2f214c50e82aa8edbb6ad"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794669493394,"sessionID":"ses_e255ff46fffexTncZnylD3HfiJ","part":{"partID":"prt_1daa33af4001XKIgVAu0R2oz6Q","sessionID":"ses_e255ff46fffexTncZnylD3HfiJ","messageID":"msg_1daa3101e0015kTAkAsMQLyy8m","type":"tool","id":"call_01_srnt0t3fasg6sfi7fdid986h","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -rn \"refus\\|question\\|mention\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog --include='*.md' | grep -i \"command\\|attach\\|offer\" | head -30; ls .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/scorer-fusion/ 2>/dev/null; ls .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/ 2>/dev/null"},"output":".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/scorer-fusion/doctor-command-routing.md:31:A doctor bridge passes on its own wording, an explicit-author match on one of its manifest phrases or its runnable string. With the local embedder running it can also pass on meaning, as [command-meaning-matching.md](./command-meaning-matching.md) describes. When none passes and none was typed, the scorer asks `/doctor:helper`'s ranker whether the prompt describes a doctor problem, and if it does the command the ranker puts first passes by name when its policy is `run`, and `/doctor:helper` passes instead when that command asks first or has no bridge. A prompt that asks for a `repair path` or says `I have a problem`, with no ranked command and no passing skill, gets `/doctor:helper`. `stopped converging` counts as a wrong state beside a passing skill. Beside a passing skill this needs a wrong state or health check in the prompt, so ordinary requests that share doctor words get no doctor line. A `/doctor:` command counts as typed when the prompt starts with `/doctor:` or when it directly follows `run`, `execute`, `start`, `launch`, `use` or `kick off` outside quotes and backticks. A quoted command or a bare mid-sentence mention does not count. A typed command puts its own passing bridge first. For other prompts, a passing skill stays first and passing doctor bridges sit right after the second passing skill or the only passing skill. This keeps a doctor line within the CLI's three shown results. Doctor bridges never change skill scores. The scorer computes skill ambiguity and abstention as if no doctor bridge existed. The Python native wrapper keeps the same order. A read-only question, such as a list or show prompt, holds every candidate under the read-only confidence floor, unless the prompt names the skill, a command bridge marked `readOnly` matches one of its own phrases, or a skill-specific rule lifts it. A prompt with a no-write phrase, such as `no edits` or `just list`, gets none of these lifts. Mutating bridges stay gated.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/scorer-fusion/command-meaning-matching.md:36:A meaning pass lifts a command past the read-only confidence floor, so `commandMeaningEligible` asks the question that floor asks of a phrase match. A prompt matching `NO_EDIT_REQUEST`, such as \"no edits\", \"do not change\", \"just show\" or \"just list\", rules out every command on meaning. A read-only explainer prompt admits only a command marked `readOnly`, because running it is the answer. `readOnlyRouteAllowed` reads the same pattern, so the two gates cannot drift apart.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/scorer-fusion/command-meaning-matching.md:44:`capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs --real-embedding` scores the command corpus against this machine's skill graph and live embedder. It first checks that the embedder serves `nomic-embed-text` and that a `vec_cmd_*` table holds vectors, and it refuses `--write`. The committed baseline still comes from the fixture run, which has no real cosine.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/scorer-fusion/command-meaning-matching.md:65:| `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-meaning-anchor.vitest.ts` | Vitest | 17 cases: cosine bar, margin, stand-in refusal, eligibility and ranking rule |\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/cli-surface/skill-advisor-cli.md:36:`--json -` reads the command's JSON object from stdin instead of from argv (`runtime/skill-advisor-cli.ts`). The prompt hook and the OpenCode plugin use it, because any local user can read another process's argv from the process table. `parseCliArgs` only marks the payload. `runSkillAdvisorCli` reads stdin once, after the arguments parse, so a bad flag is refused without waiting on stdin. `--json '<object>'` and the per-parameter flags keep working.\nablation.md\nambiguity.md\nattribution.md\ncommand-meaning-matching.md\ndoctor-command-routing.md\nexecutor-delegation.md\nfive-lane-fusion.md\nprojection.md\nweights-config.md\nauto-indexing\ncli-surface\ndaemon-and-freshness\nfeature-catalog.md\nhooks-and-plugin\nlifecycle-routing\npython-compat\nscorer-fusion\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/scorer-fusion/doctor-command-routing.md:31:A doctor bridge passes on its own wording, an explicit-author match on one of its manifest phrases or its runnable string. With the local embedder running it can also pass on meaning, as [command-meaning-matching.md](./command-meaning-matching.md) describes. When none passes and none was typed, the scorer asks `/doctor:helper`'s ranker whether the prompt describes a doctor problem, and if it does the command the ranker puts first passes by name when its policy is `run`, and `/doctor:helper` passes instead when that command asks first or has no bridge. A prompt that asks for a `repair path` or says `I have a problem`, with no ranked command and no passing skill, gets `/doctor:helper`. `stopped converging` counts as a wrong state beside a passing skill. Beside a passing skill this needs a wrong state or health check in the prompt, so ordinary requests that share doctor words get no doctor line. A `/doctor:` command counts as typed when the prompt starts with `/doctor:` or when it directly follows `run`, `execute`, `start`, `launch`, `use` or `kick off` outside quotes and backticks. A quoted command or a bare mid-sentence mention does not count. A typed command puts its own passing bridge first. For other prompts, a passing skill stays first and passing doctor bridges sit right after the second passing skill or the only passing skill. This keeps a doctor line within the CLI's three shown results. Doctor bridges never change skill scores. The scorer computes skill ambiguity and abstention as if no doctor bridge existed. The Python native wrapper keeps the same order. A read-only question, such as a list or show prompt, holds every candidate under the read-only confidence floor, unless the prompt names the skill, a command bridge marked `readOnly` matches one of its own phrases, or a skill-specific rule lifts it. A prompt with a no-write phrase, such as `no edits` or `just list`, gets none of these lifts. Mutating bridges stay gated.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/scorer-fusion/command-meaning-matching.md:36:A meaning pass lifts a command past the read-only confidence floor, so `commandMeaningEligible` asks the question that floor asks of a phrase match. A prompt matching `NO_EDIT_REQUEST`, such as \"no edits\", \"do not change\", \"just show\" or \"just list\", rules out every command on meaning. A read-only explainer prompt admits only a command marked `readOnly`, because running it is the answer. `readOnlyRouteAllowed` reads the same pattern, so the two gates cannot drift apart.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/scorer-fusion/command-meaning-matching.md:44:`capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs --real-embedding` scores the command corpus against this machine's skill graph and live embedder. It first checks that the embedder serves `nomic-embed-text` and that a `vec_cmd_*` table holds vectors, and it refuses `--write`. The committed baseline still comes from the fixture run, which has no real cosine.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/scorer-fusion/command-meaning-matching.md:65:| `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-meaning-anchor.vitest.ts` | Vitest | 17 cases: cosine bar, margin, stand-in refusal, eligibility and ranking rule |\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/cli-surface/skill-advisor-cli.md:36:`--json -` reads the command's JSON object from stdin instead of from argv (`runtime/skill-advisor-cli.ts`). The prompt hook and the OpenCode plugin use it, because any local user can read another process's argv from the process table. `parseCliArgs` only marks the payload. `runSkillAdvisorCli` reads stdin once, after the arguments parse, so a bad flag is refused without waiting on stdin. `--json '<object>'` and the per-parameter flags keep working.\nablation.md\nambiguity.md\nattribution.md\ncommand-meaning-matching.md\ndoctor-command-routing.md\nexecutor-delegation.md\nfive-lane-fusion.md\nprojection.md\nweights-config.md\nauto-indexing\ncli-surface\ndaemon-and-freshness\nfeature-catalog.md\nhooks-and-plugin\nlifecycle-routing\npython-compat\nscorer-fusion\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"grep -rn \\\"refus\\\\|question\\\\|mention\\\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog --include='*.md' | grep -i \\\"command\\\\|attach\\\\|offer\\\" | head -30; ls .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/scorer-fusion/ 2>/dev/null; ls .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/ 2>/dev/null\"}"},"time":{"start":1794669492980,"end":1794669493394}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794669494226,"sessionID":"ses_e255ff46fffexTncZnylD3HfiJ","part":{"partID":"prt_1daa33af1002Bgh8aBSOj27921","sessionID":"ses_e255ff46fffexTncZnylD3HfiJ","messageID":"msg_1daa3101e0015kTAkAsMQLyy8m","type":"tool","id":"call_00_ry4jy1kxwlxwpq8ckp5jvr2d","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -rln \"commandOnlyMentioned\\|SMALL_EDIT_WORDING\\|RETIRED_COMMANDS\\|/create:skill\\|withholdWritingCommands\\|typedCommandIn\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor --include='*.md' --include='*.mdx' --include='*.txt' | head -30"},"output":".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/changelog/v1.0.0.0.md\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/changelog/v1.1.0.0.md\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/changelog/v1.0.0.0.md\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/changelog/v1.1.0.0.md\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"grep -rln \\\"commandOnlyMentioned\\\\|SMALL_EDIT_WORDING\\\\|RETIRED_COMMANDS\\\\|/create:skill\\\\|withholdWritingCommands\\\\|typedCommandIn\\\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor --include='*.md' --include='*.mdx' --include='*.txt' | head -30\"}"},"time":{"start":1794669492977,"end":1794669494226}}}}
