{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794670037357,"sessionID":"ses_e255ff46fffexTncZnylD3HfiJ","part":{"partID":"prt_1daab88d70010fQEr2paWlfmM5","sessionID":"ses_e255ff46fffexTncZnylD3HfiJ","messageID":"msg_1daab7b6e001bLBXynOEwT3Vsw","type":"tool","id":"call_00_j4wv2swr75bkweb9ax1fbf9b","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"sed -n '40,60p' .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/README.md; grep -rn \"mutating\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/README.md | head"},"output":".skilled/commands/doctor/_routes.yaml\nhub mode-registry.json files\n                         |\n                         v\n              derive-command-bridges.cjs\n                         |\n                         v\n              command-bridges.generated.json\n```\n\nThe deriver also updates generated projection blocks in the live TypeScript and Python scoring sources. Its `GENERATED COMMAND ROWS` block in `lib/command-attach.ts` holds `COMMAND_ROWS`, one row per id a scorer can return for an enabled entry, covering the entry id and every enabled runtime id. Each row carries `command`, the runnable slash command, `policy`, its derived policy, `mutating`, the class that says what a run writes (`read-only`, `add-only` or `mutates`) when the record names one, and `ownerSkillId`, the owning skill. It writes the `MODE_COMMANDS` block into the same file, one row per hub mode that names a command, and fails when a mode names a command with no file under `.skilled/commands/`. The same block also holds `TRACKED_COMMANDS`, one row per tracked command keyed by its runnable string, emitted for every bridge entry whether or not the bridge is active. A surface that attaches a command reads its policy from `TRACKED_COMMANDS`. Each TypeScript bridge in `lib/scorer/projection.ts` carries an `ownerSkill`, the skill that owns its command. The generated JSON and generated source blocks are outputs, not hand-edited inputs.\n\nEvery tracked row carries the command's derived policy, the skill that owns it and `scorerRow`, which is true when the TypeScript scorer has a row for that entry. A typed command therefore always has a policy and an owner to deliver, even where no scorer row exists.\n\nEach bridge row with TypeScript enabled also carries an `embeddingText`, the plain sentence a scoring vector is built from. A Python-only row carries none. A metadata row leads with its `userIntent.job` sentence and adds every `userIntent.ownedSignals` phrase, because both are written in the asker's voice. The entry `description` stands in for the job only when the record states no job. A doctor row builds it from the router frontmatter `description` and the `trigger_phrases` of the route. The deriver drops each slash command string before the parts are joined, because a name left in would hand the scorer a second copy of the token it already matches literally. The field is derived on every run and never authored, so edit the sources rather than the JSON.\n\n---\n\n## 4. DOCTOR BRIDGES\n\nIds are `command-doctor-<command>-<target>` when the doctor command owns more than one route, and `command-doctor-<command>` otherwise. `skillId` and `ownerMode` come from the router's `skill_agent` tag. A router without the tag fails the deriver with a named error.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/README.md:50:The deriver also updates generated projection blocks in the live TypeScript and Python scoring sources. Its `GENERATED COMMAND ROWS` block in `lib/command-attach.ts` holds `COMMAND_ROWS`, one row per id a scorer can return for an enabled entry, covering the entry id and every enabled runtime id. Each row carries `command`, the runnable slash command, `policy`, its derived policy, `mutating`, the class that says what a run writes (`read-only`, `add-only` or `mutates`) when the record names one, and `ownerSkillId`, the owning skill. It writes the `MODE_COMMANDS` block into the same file, one row per hub mode that names a command, and fails when a mode names a command with no file under `.skilled/commands/`. The same block also holds `TRACKED_COMMANDS`, one row per tracked command keyed by its runnable string, emitted for every bridge entry whether or not the bridge is active. A surface that attaches a command reads its policy from `TRACKED_COMMANDS`. Each TypeScript bridge in `lib/scorer/projection.ts` carries an `ownerSkill`, the skill that owns its command. The generated JSON and generated source blocks are outputs, not hand-edited inputs.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/README.md:82:- TypeScript: the `userIntent.ownedSignals` phrases become the keywords and intent signals, the command's namespace and `command` become the domains, and a `read-only` `mutating` class sets `readOnly: true`.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/README.md:113:| `mutating` | `read-only`, `add-only`, `mutates` | Every metadata entry and every doctor route or action |\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/README.md:124:| 3 | `mutating` is `read-only` | `run` |\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/README.md:125:| 4 | `mutating` is `add-only` with `write_scope` set to `spec-folder` | `run` |\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/README.md:128:`commandPolicies` in `command-bridges.generated.json` holds one record per command, or one record per route or action for a doctor command. Each record carries five keys: `command`, `route`, `mutating`, `askFirst`, `policy`. Nothing scores on it yet.\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":".skilled/commands/doctor/_routes.yaml\nhub mode-registry.json files\n                         |\n                         v\n              derive-command-bridges.cjs\n                         |\n                         v\n              command-bridges.generated.json\n```\n\nThe deriver also updates generated projection blocks in the live TypeScript and Python scoring sources. Its `GENERATED COMMAND ROWS` block in `lib/command-attach.ts` holds `COMMAND_ROWS`, one row per id a scorer can return for an enabled entry, covering the entry id and every enabled runtime id. Each row carries `command`, the runnable slash command, `policy`, its derived policy, `mutating`, the class that says what a run writes (`read-only`, `add-only` or `mutates`) when the record names one, and `ownerSkillId`, the owning skill. It writes the `MODE_COMMANDS` block into the same file, one row per hub mode that names a command, and fails when a mode names a command with no file under `.skilled/commands/`. The same block also holds `TRACKED_COMMANDS`, one row per tracked command keyed by its runnable string, emitted for every bridge entry whether or not the bridge is active. A surface that attaches a command reads its policy from `TRACKED_COMMANDS`. Each TypeScript bridge in `lib/scorer/projection.ts` carries an `ownerSkill`, the skill that owns its command. The generated JSON and generated source blocks are outputs, not hand-edited inputs.\n\nEvery tracked row carries the command's derived policy, the skill that owns it and `scorerRow`, which is true when the TypeScript scorer has a row for that entry. A typed command therefore always has a policy and an owner to deliver, even where no scorer row exists.\n\nEach bridge row with TypeScript enabled also carries an `embeddingText`, the plain sentence a scoring vector is built from. A Python-only row carries none. A metadata row leads with its `userIntent.job` sentence and adds every `userIntent.ownedSignals` phrase, because both are written in the asker's voice. The entry `description` stands in for the job only when the record states no job. A doctor row builds it from the router frontmatter `description` and the `trigger_phrases` of the route. The deriver drops each slash command string before the parts are joined, because a name left in would hand the scorer a second copy of the token it already matches literally. The field is derived on every run and never authored, so edit the sources rather than the JSON.\n\n---\n\n## 4. DOCTOR BRIDGES\n\nIds are `command-doctor-<command>-<target>` when the doctor command owns more than one route, and `command-doctor-<command>` otherwise. `skillId` and `ownerMode` come from the router's `skill_agent` tag. A router without the tag fails the deriver with a named error.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/README.md:50:The deriver also updates generated projection blocks in the live TypeScript and Python scoring sources. Its `GENERATED COMMAND ROWS` block in `lib/command-attach.ts` holds `COMMAND_ROWS`, one row per id a scorer can return for an enabled entry, covering the entry id and every enabled runtime id. Each row carries `command`, the runnable slash command, `policy`, its derived policy, `mutating`, the class that says what a run writes (`read-only`, `add-only` or `mutates`) when the record names one, and `ownerSkillId`, the owning skill. It writes the `MODE_COMMANDS` block into the same file, one row per hub mode that names a command, and fails when a mode names a command with no file under `.skilled/commands/`. The same block also holds `TRACKED_COMMANDS`, one row per tracked command keyed by its runnable string, emitted for every bridge entry whether or not the bridge is active. A surface that attaches a command reads its policy from `TRACKED_COMMANDS`. Each TypeScript bridge in `lib/scorer/projection.ts` carries an `ownerSkill`, the skill that owns its command. The generated JSON and generated source blocks are outputs, not hand-edited inputs.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/README.md:82:- TypeScript: the `userIntent.ownedSignals` phrases become the keywords and intent signals, the command's namespace and `command` become the domains, and a `read-only` `mutating` class sets `readOnly: true`.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/README.md:113:| `mutating` | `read-only`, `add-only`, `mutates` | Every metadata entry and every doctor route or action |\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/README.md:124:| 3 | `mutating` is `read-only` | `run` |\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/README.md:125:| 4 | `mutating` is `add-only` with `write_scope` set to `spec-folder` | `run` |\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/README.md:128:`commandPolicies` in `command-bridges.generated.json` holds one record per command, or one record per route or action for a doctor command. Each record carries five keys: `command`, `route`, `mutating`, `askFirst`, `policy`. Nothing scores on it yet.\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"sed -n '40,60p' .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/README.md; grep -rn \\\"mutating\\\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/README.md | head\"}"},"time":{"start":1794670037207,"end":1794670037357}}}}
