{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794670048823,"sessionID":"ses_e255ff46fffexTncZnylD3HfiJ","part":{"partID":"prt_1daabb5d0002uH70PKQ31pdgI6","sessionID":"ses_e255ff46fffexTncZnylD3HfiJ","messageID":"msg_1daabad21001pZ3sRodpGoOyLZ","type":"tool","id":"call_00_a6q0c3p5fmtyl3aip34jmmxd","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -n \"command\\|typed\\|no-edit\\|policy\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md | head -40; grep -n \"command\\|typed\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/README.md | head -30"},"output":"3:description: \"Native command that returns prompt-safe skill recommendations with lane attribution, lifecycle metadata, fail-open freshness semantics and an optional compiled-routing enrichment.\"\n17:Expose the native scoring pipeline as a command that any runtime can call through the CLI front door, with prompt-safe attribution and lifecycle-aware redirects.\n23:`handlers/advisor-recommend.ts` implements the command. Input is validated by `schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts` (Zod strict). The public response always includes the resolved `workspaceRoot` plus `effectiveThresholds`, where `effectiveThresholds` publishes the active `confidenceThreshold`, `uncertaintyThreshold` and `confidenceOnly` mode after request overrides are merged with defaults. Output also carries prompt-safe `recommendations[]`, optional `laneBreakdown[]` when `includeAttribution: true`, lifecycle redirect metadata (`redirectFrom`, `redirectTo`, `status`), `freshness`, `trustState`, `generatedAt`, `cache`, optional `warnings` and optional `abstainReasons`. Fail-open states such as disabled or absent freshness still preserve `workspaceRoot` and `effectiveThresholds` in the envelope so callers can inspect the resolved repository scope and active routing thresholds even when no recommendations are returned. The handler enforces sanitization at the envelope boundary (see `lib/derived/sanitizer.ts`) and never echoes prompt text into response metadata. Cache behavior is tied to generation (see [`daemon-and-freshness/cache-invalidation.md`](../../feature-catalog/daemon-and-freshness/cache-invalidation.md)).\n33:When a recommendation gets a `compiledRoute`, `enrichCompiledRoutes()` also asks `attachCommand()` in `lib/command-attach.ts` for a slash command and sets the optional `command` field when one comes back (the `command` field of `AdvisorRecommendationSchema` in `schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts`, `z.string().min(1).optional()`). `attachCommand()` names a command only for a `route` decision with `selectionKind: 'single'` and exactly one target, and only when the generated `MODE_COMMANDS` table maps that hub and `workflowMode` to a command. A bundle, a multi-target route or a mode with no command leaves `command` off. For `system-deep-loop` the prompt must type the same command or hold a phrase from `DEPTH_WORDING`, so a prompt that only names a topic such as research or review gets no deep-loop command. A prompt that matches `NO_EDIT_REQUEST` gets no command from any hub, because a reader who rules out a change is never handed work that writes.\n35:The request option `options.includeCompiledRoute` is an optional boolean that defaults to true. When a caller passes `false` the handler skips `enrichCompiledRoutes()` on both the fresh and the cached return (the two `input.options?.includeCompiledRoute === false` checks in `handlers/advisor-recommend.ts`), so no `compiled-route.cjs` subprocess starts and no recommendation carries `compiledRoute` or `command`. The prompt hooks pass `true` (`hooks/lib/skill-advisor-cli-fallback.ts:238`), because the `command` field is derived from the compiled route. The OpenCode plugin does not pass the option and keeps receiving `compiledRoute`. The option is declared in the zod schema (the `options` of `AdvisorRecommendInputSchema` in `schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts`), the tool descriptor (`tools/advisor-recommend.ts:23`) and the CLI manifest (`skill-advisor-cli-manifest.ts:57`).\n37:The request option `options.compiledRouteTopOnly` is an optional boolean that defaults to false. When a caller passes `true`, `enrichCompiledRoutes()` runs the route lookup for the first recommendation only and returns every later one unchanged, so only the top pick can carry `compiledRoute` or `command`. Each lookup is a `compiled-route.cjs` subprocess, so this saves one subprocess for each further eligible hub. The prompt hooks pass `true` with `includeCompiledRoute: true`, because the brief leads with the top pick. The option is declared in the zod schema (the `options` of `AdvisorRecommendInputSchema` in `schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts`), the tool descriptor (`tools/advisor-recommend.ts:24`) and the CLI manifest (`skill-advisor-cli-manifest.ts:58`).\n39:A hub also leaves `command` off when the same command already ranks in the result through its own command row. `enrichCompiledRoutes()` takes the `command` fields present in the result as the commands already offered, so one command never takes two slots.\n41:### Recommendation Contract (`kind`, `command`, `policy`, `ownerSkillId`)\n43:Each recommendation carries up to four optional fields that let a reader act on it without a second lookup. `kind` is `'skill'` or `'command'` and says which face the pick is, so a caller can tell a skill entry from a command row that scored as a candidate. `command` is the runnable slash string, such as `/speckit:save`. `policy` is `'run'`, `'start'` or `'ask'` and says how a caller may run that command. `ownerSkillId` names the skill that owns the command, which is the grouping key when several commands belong to one hub. All four stay optional in `AdvisorRecommendationSchema` so a response from a runtime that predates them still parses.\n45:`lib/command-attach.ts` is the single source for the values. Its generated `COMMAND_ROWS` block maps each id a scorer can return, whether the TypeScript id, the Python id or the entry id, to `{ command, policy?, mutating?, ownerSkillId }`, and `commandRowFor(id)` reads it with an own-key check so an inherited name such as `constructor` never resolves to a row. `mutating` is the class the renderer's child downgrade reads: `/doctor:env list` carries `read-only` and stays fully visible to a dispatched child, while `start`, `ask` and a writing `run` are marked as parent-named. `publicRecommendation()` in `handlers/advisor-recommend.ts` looks one up for the recommendation's own id and copies its `command`, its `policy` when present and its `ownerSkillId`, and it forwards the scorer's `kind`. An id with no row adds none of the three. A command attached through the compiled route takes its `policy` from `TRACKED_COMMANDS[command].policy`, the table keyed by runnable string that covers every tracked command, and it takes no `ownerSkillId` because the recommendation already names the hub.\n47:The renderer and the degraded path read the same contract. `lib/render.ts` builds the brief label `<ownerSkillId or skill> → <command> (<policy>)` from the recommendation's own fields first and falls back to the generated row for anything omitted, so a hook that predates the fields still prints a runnable command. The CLI degraded path in `skill-advisor-cli.ts` emits a `kind: 'command'` entry with all four fields when the prompt contains the row's bare slash command, and otherwise emits the row's `ownerSkillId` as a `kind: 'skill'` entry so the fallback brief names a real skill instead of a raw row id.\n60:| `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts` | Shared | `attachCommand()`, the generated `MODE_COMMANDS` table and the `DEPTH_WORDING` gate that name the slash command for a single routed mode. |\n69:| `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/handlers/advisor-recommend-compiled-route-option.vitest.ts` | Automated test | `includeCompiledRoute: false` skips enrichment on fresh and cached returns. `compiledRouteTopOnly: true` looks up the first recommendation alone. A command that ranks through its own row is not offered again by its hub. |\n18:`runtime/` owns the standalone advisor package: the daemon that serves the CLI, the scorer, the skill graph and the SQLite state. It exposes 9 public commands across two families: advisor routing (`advisor_*`) and skill graph queries (`skill_graph_*`).\n22:- `advisor-server.ts` is the advisor daemon entrypoint. It serves the IPC socket (`startIpcSocketServer`), dispatches command frames into `tools/`, manages daemon lifecycle and triggers skill metadata indexing on startup. Its caller-context builder resolves trust **fail-closed**: a caller that asserts no authority is untrusted, and only `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_TRUST_DEFAULT=trusted` in the daemon's own environment (unforgeable by callers) restores a default-trusted posture for maintenance flows.\n23:- `skill-advisor-cli.ts` (with `skill-advisor-cli-manifest.ts`) is the daemon-backed CLI over the same 9 commands, fronted by the `.skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs` shim: the single front door. Calls are sent untrusted by default; `--trusted` / `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_CLI_TRUSTED=1` marks maintainer mutations (`advisor_rebuild`, `skill_graph_scan`, apply-mode `skill_graph_propagate_enhances`), and the gate fails closed with a usage error (exit `64`) otherwise. Shared exit taxonomy `0`/`1`/`64`/`69`/`75`; `--warm-only` probes and exits `75` instead of cold-spawning the daemon.\n24:- `tools/` defines command descriptors and dispatches calls. `tools/index.ts` registers `TOOL_DEFINITIONS` with 4 advisor commands plus the spread of skill-graph commands.\n206:| `advisor-server.ts` | Advisor daemon entrypoint: IPC socket server, command dispatch, daemon startup, skill graph indexing via `indexSkillMetadata`, and the fail-closed trusted-caller resolution (`resolveTrustedCaller` honors `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_TRUST_DEFAULT=trusted` from the daemon env only). |\n208:| `skill-advisor-cli-manifest.ts` | Hand-maintained CLI tool manifest asserted at CLI startup and covered by the manifest parity suite so command schemas stay byte-identical to the tool registry. |\n266:│ typed command response                   │\n274:The runtime defines 9 public commands (4 advisor + 5 skill_graph) in `tools/index.ts:37-43` and `tools/skill-graph-tools.ts:22,35,55,61,67`; all nine are reached through the `skill-advisor` CLI.\n278:| `advisor-server.ts` | Daemon | Advisor daemon entrypoint: serves the IPC socket, dispatches commands to `tools/`, manages daemon lifecycle. |\n288:| `node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs <command>` | CLI | Daemon-backed front door for all 9 commands (shim guards dist freshness, exit `69`; `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_CLI_DEV_ALLOW_STALE=1` dev override; `list-tools` answers offline; `--trusted` for maintainer mutations). |\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"3:description: \"Native command that returns prompt-safe skill recommendations with lane attribution, lifecycle metadata, fail-open freshness semantics and an optional compiled-routing enrichment.\"\n17:Expose the native scoring pipeline as a command that any runtime can call through the CLI front door, with prompt-safe attribution and lifecycle-aware redirects.\n23:`handlers/advisor-recommend.ts` implements the command. Input is validated by `schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts` (Zod strict). The public response always includes the resolved `workspaceRoot` plus `effectiveThresholds`, where `effectiveThresholds` publishes the active `confidenceThreshold`, `uncertaintyThreshold` and `confidenceOnly` mode after request overrides are merged with defaults. Output also carries prompt-safe `recommendations[]`, optional `laneBreakdown[]` when `includeAttribution: true`, lifecycle redirect metadata (`redirectFrom`, `redirectTo`, `status`), `freshness`, `trustState`, `generatedAt`, `cache`, optional `warnings` and optional `abstainReasons`. Fail-open states such as disabled or absent freshness still preserve `workspaceRoot` and `effectiveThresholds` in the envelope so callers can inspect the resolved repository scope and active routing thresholds even when no recommendations are returned. The handler enforces sanitization at the envelope boundary (see `lib/derived/sanitizer.ts`) and never echoes prompt text into response metadata. Cache behavior is tied to generation (see [`daemon-and-freshness/cache-invalidation.md`](../../feature-catalog/daemon-and-freshness/cache-invalidation.md)).\n33:When a recommendation gets a `compiledRoute`, `enrichCompiledRoutes()` also asks `attachCommand()` in `lib/command-attach.ts` for a slash command and sets the optional `command` field when one comes back (the `command` field of `AdvisorRecommendationSchema` in `schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts`, `z.string().min(1).optional()`). `attachCommand()` names a command only for a `route` decision with `selectionKind: 'single'` and exactly one target, and only when the generated `MODE_COMMANDS` table maps that hub and `workflowMode` to a command. A bundle, a multi-target route or a mode with no command leaves `command` off. For `system-deep-loop` the prompt must type the same command or hold a phrase from `DEPTH_WORDING`, so a prompt that only names a topic such as research or review gets no deep-loop command. A prompt that matches `NO_EDIT_REQUEST` gets no command from any hub, because a reader who rules out a change is never handed work that writes.\n35:The request option `options.includeCompiledRoute` is an optional boolean that defaults to true. When a caller passes `false` the handler skips `enrichCompiledRoutes()` on both the fresh and the cached return (the two `input.options?.includeCompiledRoute === false` checks in `handlers/advisor-recommend.ts`), so no `compiled-route.cjs` subprocess starts and no recommendation carries `compiledRoute` or `command`. The prompt hooks pass `true` (`hooks/lib/skill-advisor-cli-fallback.ts:238`), because the `command` field is derived from the compiled route. The OpenCode plugin does not pass the option and keeps receiving `compiledRoute`. The option is declared in the zod schema (the `options` of `AdvisorRecommendInputSchema` in `schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts`), the tool descriptor (`tools/advisor-recommend.ts:23`) and the CLI manifest (`skill-advisor-cli-manifest.ts:57`).\n37:The request option `options.compiledRouteTopOnly` is an optional boolean that defaults to false. When a caller passes `true`, `enrichCompiledRoutes()` runs the route lookup for the first recommendation only and returns every later one unchanged, so only the top pick can carry `compiledRoute` or `command`. Each lookup is a `compiled-route.cjs` subprocess, so this saves one subprocess for each further eligible hub. The prompt hooks pass `true` with `includeCompiledRoute: true`, because the brief leads with the top pick. The option is declared in the zod schema (the `options` of `AdvisorRecommendInputSchema` in `schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts`), the tool descriptor (`tools/advisor-recommend.ts:24`) and the CLI manifest (`skill-advisor-cli-manifest.ts:58`).\n39:A hub also leaves `command` off when the same command already ranks in the result through its own command row. `enrichCompiledRoutes()` takes the `command` fields present in the result as the commands already offered, so one command never takes two slots.\n41:### Recommendation Contract (`kind`, `command`, `policy`, `ownerSkillId`)\n43:Each recommendation carries up to four optional fields that let a reader act on it without a second lookup. `kind` is `'skill'` or `'command'` and says which face the pick is, so a caller can tell a skill entry from a command row that scored as a candidate. `command` is the runnable slash string, such as `/speckit:save`. `policy` is `'run'`, `'start'` or `'ask'` and says how a caller may run that command. `ownerSkillId` names the skill that owns the command, which is the grouping key when several commands belong to one hub. All four stay optional in `AdvisorRecommendationSchema` so a response from a runtime that predates them still parses.\n45:`lib/command-attach.ts` is the single source for the values. Its generated `COMMAND_ROWS` block maps each id a scorer can return, whether the TypeScript id, the Python id or the entry id, to `{ command, policy?, mutating?, ownerSkillId }`, and `commandRowFor(id)` reads it with an own-key check so an inherited name such as `constructor` never resolves to a row. `mutating` is the class the renderer's child downgrade reads: `/doctor:env list` carries `read-only` and stays fully visible to a dispatched child, while `start`, `ask` and a writing `run` are marked as parent-named. `publicRecommendation()` in `handlers/advisor-recommend.ts` looks one up for the recommendation's own id and copies its `command`, its `policy` when present and its `ownerSkillId`, and it forwards the scorer's `kind`. An id with no row adds none of the three. A command attached through the compiled route takes its `policy` from `TRACKED_COMMANDS[command].policy`, the table keyed by runnable string that covers every tracked command, and it takes no `ownerSkillId` because the recommendation already names the hub.\n47:The renderer and the degraded path read the same contract. `lib/render.ts` builds the brief label `<ownerSkillId or skill> → <command> (<policy>)` from the recommendation's own fields first and falls back to the generated row for anything omitted, so a hook that predates the fields still prints a runnable command. The CLI degraded path in `skill-advisor-cli.ts` emits a `kind: 'command'` entry with all four fields when the prompt contains the row's bare slash command, and otherwise emits the row's `ownerSkillId` as a `kind: 'skill'` entry so the fallback brief names a real skill instead of a raw row id.\n60:| `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts` | Shared | `attachCommand()`, the generated `MODE_COMMANDS` table and the `DEPTH_WORDING` gate that name the slash command for a single routed mode. |\n69:| `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/handlers/advisor-recommend-compiled-route-option.vitest.ts` | Automated test | `includeCompiledRoute: false` skips enrichment on fresh and cached returns. `compiledRouteTopOnly: true` looks up the first recommendation alone. A command that ranks through its own row is not offered again by its hub. |\n18:`runtime/` owns the standalone advisor package: the daemon that serves the CLI, the scorer, the skill graph and the SQLite state. It exposes 9 public commands across two families: advisor routing (`advisor_*`) and skill graph queries (`skill_graph_*`).\n22:- `advisor-server.ts` is the advisor daemon entrypoint. It serves the IPC socket (`startIpcSocketServer`), dispatches command frames into `tools/`, manages daemon lifecycle and triggers skill metadata indexing on startup. Its caller-context builder resolves trust **fail-closed**: a caller that asserts no authority is untrusted, and only `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_TRUST_DEFAULT=trusted` in the daemon's own environment (unforgeable by callers) restores a default-trusted posture for maintenance flows.\n23:- `skill-advisor-cli.ts` (with `skill-advisor-cli-manifest.ts`) is the daemon-backed CLI over the same 9 commands, fronted by the `.skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs` shim: the single front door. Calls are sent untrusted by default; `--trusted` / `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_CLI_TRUSTED=1` marks maintainer mutations (`advisor_rebuild`, `skill_graph_scan`, apply-mode `skill_graph_propagate_enhances`), and the gate fails closed with a usage error (exit `64`) otherwise. Shared exit taxonomy `0`/`1`/`64`/`69`/`75`; `--warm-only` probes and exits `75` instead of cold-spawning the daemon.\n24:- `tools/` defines command descriptors and dispatches calls. `tools/index.ts` registers `TOOL_DEFINITIONS` with 4 advisor commands plus the spread of skill-graph commands.\n206:| `advisor-server.ts` | Advisor daemon entrypoint: IPC socket server, command dispatch, daemon startup, skill graph indexing via `indexSkillMetadata`, and the fail-closed trusted-caller resolution (`resolveTrustedCaller` honors `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_TRUST_DEFAULT=trusted` from the daemon env only). |\n208:| `skill-advisor-cli-manifest.ts` | Hand-maintained CLI tool manifest asserted at CLI startup and covered by the manifest parity suite so command schemas stay byte-identical to the tool registry. |\n266:│ typed command response                   │\n274:The runtime defines 9 public commands (4 advisor + 5 skill_graph) in `tools/index.ts:37-43` and `tools/skill-graph-tools.ts:22,35,55,61,67`; all nine are reached through the `skill-advisor` CLI.\n278:| `advisor-server.ts` | Daemon | Advisor daemon entrypoint: serves the IPC socket, dispatches commands to `tools/`, manages daemon lifecycle. |\n288:| `node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs <command>` | CLI | Daemon-backed front door for all 9 commands (shim guards dist freshness, exit `69`; `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_CLI_DEV_ALLOW_STALE=1` dev override; `list-tools` answers offline; `--trusted` for maintainer mutations). |\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"grep -n \\\"command\\\\|typed\\\\|no-edit\\\\|policy\\\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md | head -40; grep -n \\\"command\\\\|typed\\\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/README.md | head -30\"}"},"time":{"start":1794670048720,"end":1794670048823}}}}
